{
  "id": "merge-intervals-ii-with-count",
  "questionId": "merge-intervals-ii-with-count",
  "slug": "merge-intervals-ii-with-count",
  "title": "Maximum Overlapping Intervals",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Merge intervals",
  "patterns": [
    "merge intervals",
    "sweep line",
    "sorting"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "the maximum overlap can only change at a point where some interval starts or ends - you don't need to check every real number, just those event points",
    "turn each interval into two events: a +1 event at start, and a -1 event at end + 1 (since endpoints are inclusive, the interval stops covering points only after end)",
    "sort all events by their position. when positions tie, process all the +1 (start) events at that position before any -1 (end) events, so intervals that share an endpoint count as overlapping there",
    "sweep through the sorted events maintaining a running counter (add on +1 events, subtract on -1 events), and track the maximum value the counter reaches"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using end instead of end + 1 for the closing event, which incorrectly excludes the interval from counting at its own last covered point",
    "processing end events before start events at the same coordinate, which undercounts intervals that share exactly one point",
    "only checking interval start points as candidate maxima, which can miss the true peak in some overlap patterns"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of `intervals`, each represented as `[start, end]`\nusing inclusive integer endpoints.\n\n**Return the maximum number of intervals that all overlap at some single point in time, i.e. the highest number of intervals that simultaneously contain some common value.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "each interval is [start, end] inclusive, with start <= end",
      "the input is not guaranteed to be sorted",
      "the list contains at least one interval"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single interval (answer 1)",
      "no intervals overlap at all (answer 1)",
      "every interval overlaps at one common point",
      "intervals that only touch at a single shared endpoint",
      "intervals of length zero, i.e. start == end"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
